<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hutsuls</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hutsuls are a Ukrainian-speaking highland people of the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-mountains">Carpathians</a>, in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>. Traditionally herders, woodcutters and rafters, they are known for their wooden churches, embroidery, woodcarving and music. In 1918-1919 Hutsuls around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yasinia">Yasinia</a> briefly set up their own local republic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hutsuls.jpg" alt="Hutsuls" width="320" height="315" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Henryk Gąsiorowski · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hutsul_famy_from_Verkhovyna,_Ukraine.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Hutsuls are an East Slavic people. Their territory, Hutsulshchyna, takes in parts of the historical regions of Halychyna, Bukovyna and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-ruthenia">Zakarpattia</a> and the basins of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prut">Prut</a>, Cheremosh and Seret rivers. The origin of the name is uncertain. It is first attested in 1816 and is thought to be an exonym used by neighbouring groups.</p><p>Like all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rusyns">Rusyns</a>, the Hutsuls most probably have a diverse ethnogenetic origin. They are generally considered descendants of the White Croats, the Tivertsi and possibly the Ulichs, who had to leave their earlier home near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-bug">Southern Bug</a> under pressure from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a>. A relation to Vlach shepherds who later came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> is also suggested. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> they have often been officially treated as a subgroup of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukrainians">Ukrainians</a>, while some scholars and some Hutsuls regard them as their own nation or as part of the Rusyn nation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first historical information on the Hutsuls comes from Polish chronicles of the 14th and 15th centuries, and the settlement of Zhabye is first mentioned in 1424. Their lands saw rebellions against Polish rule, such as the Mukha uprising of 1490, and in 1648 an uprising led by Semen Vysochan, a supporter of Bohdan Khmelnytskyi, spread through the area. From the late 17th century bands of opryshky were active there, the best known led by Oleksa Dovbush of Pechenizhyn. Until the 1770s Hutsulshchyna was divided between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, Turkish Moldavia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. Under Austrian rule the most important uprising against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a>, in the 1840s, was led by Lukian Kobylytsia.</p><p>A Hutsul-Bukovyna Legion of the Austrian army was created in 1915-1916, and in 1918-1920 Hutsuls took an active part in the Ukrainian War of Independence; in 1919 a Hutsul kurin fought in the XI (Sambir) brigade of the Ukrainian Galician Army. In the Romanian census of 1930 there were 12,456 Hutsuls in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> within its borders of that time.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Hutsul society was traditionally based on forestry and logging and on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and sheep breeding, and the Hutsuls are credited with creating the Hucul pony. Their emblematic tool is the shepherd's axe, the bartka, a small axe with a long handle, often carved and handed down at marriage. Permanent homes, the grazhda, were built at up to 1,700 metres above sea level as fortified places, and several generations of a family could live in one. The Hutsuls are also known for wooden churches, as at Kryvorivnia, Vorokhta and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yasinia">Yasinia</a>.</p><p>Their crafts include clothing, woodworking, brass metalwork, rug weaving, Kosiv pottery and the decoration of eggs, and their instruments include the trembita, a kind of alpenhorn, and the sopilka fife. Most Hutsuls belong to the Ukrainian Greek Catholic Church or the Ukrainian Orthodox Church. Their culture inspired writers such as Ivan Franko and Lesya Ukrainka.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hutsuls” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In November 1918, as Hungarian rule over Transcarpathia collapsed, a council of <b>Hutsuls</b> in the mountain village of Yasinia took power locally.</blockquote><a href="/state/hutsul-republic">Hutsul Republic</a></li></ul></section>
